  * the backing server-side (the disclosure floor), so the caller MUST mint at
  * vkAllocateMemory time, before any device write to mem_id -- a lazy mint after
  * a GPU copy INTO the memory would zero the results (server audit F4).
- * Three-valued return (V-3b-3c-2b F2, refined F5/F6): 0 = success
- * (+ *res_id_out). 1 = failed, server slot provably NOT installed -> the caller
- * SHOULD free its handle: a wedged/ctx-less conn, an open failure (the Twrite
- * never reached the server), or the routine -ENOMEM holistic-cap refusal (the
- * Rlerror means nothing was installed). -1 = failed, slot MAYBE installed -> the
- * caller MUST keep the handle marked (else a re-handed handle collides with the
- * still-installed slot, the ring-F1 wedge): a duplicate handle -EINVAL (the slot
- * IS installed -- e.g. a concurrent-alloc race loser, F6), an -EINTR whose
- * Twrite may have committed before the ack (F5 -- LATENT until native readers
- * become caught-interruptible, the planned 11c), an info-read failure, or any
- * unrecognized errno. Freeing on 1 keeps a memory-pressure loop from burning the
- * 256-slot space on routine cap refusals; keeping on everything else preserves
- * the two-sided guest-handle <-> server-slot invariant. Requires dev9p to
- * propagate the real Rlerror ecode (t_write returns the negative errno). */
+ * Three-valued return (V-3b-3c-2b F2, refined F5/F6; F9-converged at the
+ * vkQuake-arc W-1): 0 = success (+ *res_id_out). 1 = failed, server slot
+ * provably NOT installed -> the caller SHOULD free its handle: a wedged/
+ * ctx-less conn, an open failure (the Twrite never reached the server), the
+ * routine -ENOMEM holistic-cap refusal (the Rlerror means nothing was
+ * installed), or a maybe-installed failure RESOLVED by the internal reclaim
+ * (a best-effort mem/<h>/ctl destroy whose FULL WRITE confirms the teardown,
+ * or whose -ENOENT asserts absence -- exact on the write leg, best-effort on
+ * the open leg since dev9p_walk collapses any walk failure to a manufactured
+ * -ENOENT; the residue there is one self-healing spurious alloc failure, NEVER
+ * a live-slot free -- see warp_mem_reclaim; covers E_IO device faults under
+ * global hostmem pressure [F9], -EINTR maybe-commits [F5], info-read failures).
+ * -1 = failed, keep the handle marked (else a re-handed handle collides with
+ * the still-installed slot, the ring-F1 wedge). The -1 set is now only: the
+ * duplicate handle -EINVAL (the slot IS installed and LIVE -- reclaiming it
+ * would destroy an earlier mint's memory, so it is never attempted; with the
+ * transport mutex this arm is defense-in-depth, F6/F8), or a reclaim whose
+ * own transport failed (bounded leak, ctx-death reaped). Freeing on 1 keeps
+ * a memory-pressure loop from burning the 256-slot space; keeping on -1
+ * preserves the two-sided guest-handle <-> server-slot invariant. Requires
+ * dev9p to propagate the real Rlerror ecode (t_write returns the negative
+ * errno). */
 int warp_mem_new(struct warp_conn *w, uint32_t handle, uint32_t bytes,
                  uint64_t mem_id, uint32_t *res_id_out);
 
